# Shell scripts must keep LF line endings in the WORKING COPY, not just in git.
#
# Git stores LF and a Windows checkout rewrites it to CRLF. That is harmless for
# anything only Linux CI runs — but EAS Build uploads the working tree, so a
# build started from a Windows machine shipped `set -eu\r` to a Linux shell and
# died with:
#
#     install-rust-toolchain.sh: 24: set: Illegal option -
#
# with the preceding error line visibly overwritten by the carriage return. The
# file was correct in git the whole time, which is what made it confusing.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Same reasoning: these are read by Linux tooling even when edited on Windows.
*.bash text eol=lf
Dockerfile text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
